ss one format, sorting is trivial. Maybe pass two formats to make sort meaningful: [{'url': '...a', 'ext': 'mp4'}, {'url': '...b', 'ext': 'mp4', 'tbr': 100}]. Eh, one is fine. Actually, let me pass two with different heights to verify sorting doesn't crash and formats preserved. Keep it simple: one format.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually, should the mock return formats or should I let real `_extract_m3u8_formats` run with mocked `_download_webpage_handle`? Let me reconsider. The instruction: \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" \u2014 lines 218-220 are the call itself; mocking the method still executes those lines. It's fine and much more robust. I'll mock.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 one concern: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', mock)` \u2014 since `_extract_m3u8_formats` is called as `self._extract_m3u8_formats(...)` and instance attribute shadows class method, the mock is called WITHOUT self. So signature: `def fake(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, entry_protocol=..., m3u8_id=...)`. My lambda: `lambda m3u8_url, video_id, *args, **kwargs: formats`. The call: `self._extract_m3u8_formats(m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4', entry_protocol='m3u8_native', m3u8_id='hls')` \u2192 fake(m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4', entry_protocol=..., m3u8_id=...). Good.\n\nSimilarly `_download_webpage` mock: called as `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)` \u2192 fake(url, item_id). Good.\n\nNow \u2014 cleanup/state pollution: each test creates its own IE instance and monkeypatch auto-undoes. The FakeYDL is local. No global state modified. The `youtube_dl.extractor` import may populate `youtube_dl.extractor._ALL_CLASSES` etc. \u2014 that's import-time, unavoidable, and not pollution per se.\n\nNow let me write the get_info calls:\n\n1. 'try_get'\n2. 'unified_timestamp'\n3. 'parse_duration'\n4. 'clean_html'\n5. 'urljoin'\n6. 'InfoExtractor.__init__'\n7. 'InfoExtractor.report_warning'\n\nLet me do these in one block.\n\nThen finalize the test code.\n\nActually, also let me double-check `InfoExtractor._parse_json` \u2014 already have. And `LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract` full source \u2014 the prompt gives it; get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract') could confirm exact code (e.g., whether it's `lecture_id` or `lesson_id` variable). The prompt shows `chapter_id, lecture_id, course_id = mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')`. Hmm wait, actually let me re-read the prompt code:\n\n```\n       156:         chapter_id, lecture_id, course_id = mobj.group('chapter_id', 'lesson_id', 'course_id')\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually I wonder if the prompt has a typo and the real code is `lesson_id`. Doesn't matter for testing. But let me get the real source anyway to be safe. Actually the prompt says \"The code below, extracted from youtube_dl/extractor/linuxacademy.py\" \u2014 so it IS the code. But get_info might show the real one. Let me include it in the batch.\n\nLet me also check 'LinuxAcademyIE._real_initialize' \u2014 not needed.\n\nOK, making the calls now.\n\nAfter that, write the test file:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_str  # maybe not needed\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import parse_duration, unified_timestamp\n\n\nclass FakeYDL(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for YoutubeDL: only what the extractor touches.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n\n    def report_warning(self, msg):\n        pass\n\n    def to_screen(self, msg):\n        pass\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE(FakeYDL())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', lambda url, video_id, *a, **kw: webpage)\n    if formats is None:\n        formats = [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4'}]\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', lambda *a, **kw: list(formats))\n    return ie\n```\n\nHmm wait \u2014 `LinuxAcademyIE(FakeYDL())` \u2014 check __init__ signature first.\n\nTests:\n\n```python\ndef test_course_playlist_full(monkeypatch):\n    module = {...}\n    webpage = 'window.module = %s;' % json.dumps(module)\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n    result = ie._real_extract('https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/154')\n    ...assertions...\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_course_playlist_empty_items_desc_fallback(monkeypatch):\n    module = {'title': 'Empty Course', 'desc': '<p>Plain description</p>', 'items': []}\n    webpage = 'window.module = %s;' % json.dumps(module)\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n    result = ie._real_extract('https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/999')\n    assert result == {\n        '_type': 'playlist',\n        'entries': [],\n        'id': '999',\n        'title': 'Empty Course',\n        'description': 'Plain description',\n        'duration': None,\n    }\n```\n\nWait \u2014 result dict keys: '_type', 'entries', 'id', 'title', 'description', 'duration'. Yes exactly those. So full-dict equality works. \n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_with_lesson(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/playlist.m3u8\"}];'\n        'window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"What Is Data Science\", '\n        '\"md_desc\": \"Lesson description\", '\n        '\"date\": \"2020-12-08 05:18:27\", \"duration\": \"5:04\"};'\n    )\n    captured = {}\n\n    def fake_extract(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, **kwargs):\n        captured.update(m3u8_url=m3u8_url, video_id=video_id, ext=ext, kwargs=kwargs)\n        return [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.mp4', 'ext': 'mp4'}]\n\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE(FakeYDL())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', lambda url, video_id, *a, **kw: webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract)\n\n    result = ie._real_extract('https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/7971/lesson/2')\n\n    assert captured['m3u8_url'] == 'https://example.com/hls/playlist.m3u8'\n    assert captured['video_id'] == '7971-2'\n    assert captured['ext'] == 'mp4'\n    assert captured['kwargs'] == {'entry_protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'm3u8_id': 'hls'}\n\n    assert result['id'] == '7971-2'\n    assert result['title'] == 'What Is Data Science'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Lesson description'\n    assert result['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2020-12-08 05:18:27')\n    assert result['timestamp'] is not None\n    assert result['duration'] == 304\n    assert result['formats'] == [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.mp4', 'ext': 'mp4'}]\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 the webpage string: careful with the lesson regex `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`. The JSON: `{\"lesson_name\": \"What Is Data Science\", \"md_desc\": \"Lesson description\", \"date\": \"2020-12-08 05:18:27\", \"duration\": \"5:04\"}` \u2014 any `}` inside? No nested objects. The final `}` followed by `;`. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the playlist regex `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 the webpage continues after `;` with `window.lesson...`. The `\\[.+?\\]` non-greedy: first `]` is at end of `[{\"file\": \"...\"}]`. Followed by `;`. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing: `.+?` \u2014 the JSON contains `\"` and `/` \u2014 fine. No newlines if I concatenate strings without '\\n'. I'll build with implicit concatenation (no newlines).\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_player_lesson_and_title_fallback(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/playlist.m3u8\"}];'\n        'player.lesson = {\"desc\": \"<p>Fallback description</p>\", \"created_on\": \"Dec 9, 2020\"};'\n        '<h1>Lecture: Some Lecture Title</h1>'\n    )\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n    result = ie._real_extract('https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1498/lesson/2')\n\n    assert result['id'] == '1498-2'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Some Lecture Title'\n    assert result['description'] == 'Fallback description'\n    assert result['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('Dec 9, 2020')\n    assert result['timestamp'] is not None\n    assert result['duration'] is None\n    assert result['formats'] == [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4'}]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 default formats from _make_ie: [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4'}]. Hmm, but `_sort_formats` runs on it \u2014 determine_protocol('https://...') \u2192 'https' \u2192 fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 careful with `_sort_formats` and format without 'url'... mine has url. Fine.\n\n```python\ndef test_single_video_no_lesson_title_fallback_second_pattern(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        'player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/playlist.m3u8\"}];'\n        'var lessonName = \"Another Title\";'\n    )\n    ie = _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage)\n    result = ie._real_extract('https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1498/lesson/2')\n\n    assert result['id'] == '1498-2'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Another Title'\n    assert 'description' not in result\n    assert 'timestamp' not in result\n    assert 'duration' not in result\n    assert result['formats'] == [{'url': 'https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4'}]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the second title pattern: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1`. In 'var lessonName = \"Another Title\";' \u2014 matches `lessonName = \"Another Title\"` \u2192 group 1 = '\"', value = 'Another Title', then `\\1` = '\"'. Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 the first pattern `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` \u2014 does the webpage contain '>Lecture'? No. Good.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, there's a potential issue: the playlist regex `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 in test 4's webpage, after the playlist assignment there's `var lessonName = \"Another Title\";`. The `\\[.+?\\]` matches `[{\"file\": \"https://example.com/hls/playlist.m3u8\"}]` followed by `;`. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more consideration for test